WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:15.290
THE AMAZON MEAT GRINDER:
Amazon wants their employees to resign.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:18.880
This might sound counterintuitive, but it’s
true.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:24.869
It turns out that for Amazon, it’s actually
beneficial to constantly churn through employees.

00:00:24.869 --> 00:00:29.300
This is actually one of the main reasons that
Amazon treats their employees so poorly.

00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:33.399
Whether it be exposing warehouse workers to
safety hazards, making their drivers pee in

00:00:33.399 --> 00:00:39.160
bottles, or working tech staff to the bone,
Amazon does it all and it looks like their

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:43.300
efforts of creating a terrible working environment
are playing out perfectly.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:50.030
In fact, Amazon’s turnover rate is so high
that they measure it on a weekly basis.

00:00:50.030 --> 00:00:54.930
Amazon loses approximately 3% of their warehouse
workers every single week.

00:00:54.930 --> 00:00:58.190
This translates to an annual turnover rate
of a 150%.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:05.409
In other words, Amazon’s entire warehouse
workforce is replaced basically every 8 months.

00:01:05.409 --> 00:01:10.400
And considering working conditions for these
people, this rate is not too surprising, but

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.350
why in the world would Amazon want their employees
to resign?

00:01:14.350 --> 00:01:19.619
They likely spent thousands if not tens of
thousands of dollars on recruiting each and

00:01:19.619 --> 00:01:21.650
every one of their employees.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:27.040
And let’s not forget all of the time and
resources they spend on onboarding and training

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:28.040
employees.

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:32.720
So, it doesn’t really make sense to sign
them on just for 2/3 warehouse workers to

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:37.450
leave in 90 days and their tech workers to
leave in 2 years right?

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:41.830
Wrong, it turns out that constantly churning
through employees is not only cheaper for

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:44.510
Amazon but substantially more productive.

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:50.049
In fact, Jeff Bezos has gone as far as declaring
that a tenured workforce is simply a march

00:01:50.049 --> 00:01:51.360
to mediocrity.

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:58.180
As employees become more tenured, they tend
to also become more comfortable and more lazy.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:04.770
So, over the years Amazon has perfected how
to attract employees and push employees away

00:02:04.770 --> 00:02:06.070
at the same time.

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:11.890
Essentially, they want you to come in with
big dreams, work your ass off for 2 years,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.970
realize that no one recognizes your efforts,
and then leave so that the next sucker can

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.440
come in and do the same thing.

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.860
They’ve basically mastered the art of running
a cutthroat meatgrinder, so here’s the twisted

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:31.590
economics of Amazon and the downright putrid
tactics they use to screw over the employees.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:37.140
DECEITFUL COMPENSATION:
In terms of how Amazon pushes out blue collar

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.540
workers, it’s not really rocket science.

00:02:39.540 --> 00:02:43.879
They basically create a high pressure high
stress environment with lofty targets and

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:45.060
no down time.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:48.930
But, the way they handle their tech workers
is a lot more interesting.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:53.709
As you know, tech workers demand a pretty
lofty salary, so Amazon has to employ some

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:56.810
pretty clever yet disgusting strategies.

00:02:56.810 --> 00:03:01.050
Likely the most notable of these strategies
is screwing over employees with wonky vesting

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:02.080
periods.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.780
If you’re not familiar with vesting periods,
it’s basically how much time you have to

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:09.379
wait until you can cash in on your stock compensation.

00:03:09.379 --> 00:03:14.040
Most tech companies operate under a 4 year
vesting schedule with stock compensation being

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:16.130
evenly split up across the years.

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:21.239
At Facebook, for example, employees get 25%
of their total stock grant every year, with

00:03:21.239 --> 00:03:24.519
6.25% being unlocked every quarter.

00:03:24.519 --> 00:03:27.230
Google actually takes it to another level
in terms of vesting.

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:31.860
They not only front load their stock vesting,
but they do vesting on a monthly basis, so

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:33.739
it’s almost like a monthly salary.

00:03:33.739 --> 00:03:38.480
Amazon, however, does the exact opposite as
they backload their vesting.

00:03:38.480 --> 00:03:43.050
Engineers have to wait a full year just for
5% of their stock to vest and another full

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:45.209
year for 15% to vest.

00:03:45.209 --> 00:03:50.270
It’s not until the 3rd and 4th years that
engineers actually receive 80% of their promised

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:51.270
stock.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:55.830
This might not sound like a big deal but if
you enter Amazon at a relatively senior level,

00:03:55.830 --> 00:03:57.190
it really screws you over.

00:03:57.190 --> 00:04:02.629
For example, let’s say you have the option
to enter Google as an L6 and Amazon as an

00:04:02.629 --> 00:04:06.989
L7 which are approximately equal positions.

00:04:06.989 --> 00:04:12.629
On paper, both companies pay approximately
the same amount, just over $500,000.

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:14.780
Amazon actually pays $30,000 more.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:19.700
But, if you made your decision based on this
bottom line number, you’d be making a mistake,

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:20.720
and here’s why.

00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:26.500
This stock grant number of $318,000 is not
something that you receive every year, it’s

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:29.760
just an average of what you’ll receive over
the next 4 years.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.280
So, Amazon will basically offer you $1.27
million for the next 4 years and it vests

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:36.940
according to this schedule.

00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:41.050
The same thing applies to Google, but given
that they have a much more fair vesting schedule,

00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.840
it’s not really to your disadvantage.

00:04:42.840 --> 00:04:46.889
In fact, here’s how both jobs breakdown
over 4 years.

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:52.690
During year one, Google pays $611,000 while
Amazon pays $306,000.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:58.639
$306,000 is obviously not a bad amount, but
it’s only half of Google despite Amazon’s

00:04:58.639 --> 00:05:02.470
compensation seeming like it was higher than
Google’s.

00:05:02.470 --> 00:05:09.259
During year two, Google will pay another $611,000
while Amazon will pay $433,000.

00:05:09.259 --> 00:05:15.150
During year three, Google drops to $506,000
while Amazon rockets to $750,000.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:20.340
And during the last year, Google drops further
to $401,000 while Amazon stays constant at

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.340
$750.

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.620
If you’re wondering why Google’s compensation
drops over the years, it’s because they’re

00:05:24.620 --> 00:05:27.400
betting the stock price will increase by the
time you receive.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:32.699
So, when you account for stock growth, your
compensation should be relatively stable.

00:05:32.699 --> 00:05:36.710
Amazon will of course also experience stock
growth, so their compensation in the last

00:05:36.710 --> 00:05:41.030
two years will likely be over a million, but
you can bet, they’re gonna do everything

00:05:41.030 --> 00:05:44.830
they can to push you out in the first two
years.

00:05:44.830 --> 00:05:50.240
THE AXE:
Statistically speaking, neither the average

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.750
Google nor the average Amazon tech worker
reaches 2 years.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:59.000
The average tenure at Google is 1.9 years
while the average tenure at Amazon is 1.84

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:00.000
years.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.110
So, not a big difference in employee retention,
but the difference in compensation is night

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:06.110
and day.

00:06:06.110 --> 00:06:10.050
Given that Google has monthly vesting, an
employee leaving at 1.9 years has actually

00:06:10.050 --> 00:06:12.250
vested over 60% of their stock.

00:06:12.250 --> 00:06:17.240
An employee at Amazon leaving at 1.84 years,
however, won’t have even vested their year

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.210
2 stock as they have to finish the year.

00:06:19.210 --> 00:06:24.080
So, they’re left with just the 5% that was
vested after year 1.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:29.710
In other words, remember that massive $1.27
million stock grant that Amazon offered when

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:35.270
you join, well, the average Amazonian will
only end up receiving a mere $63,000 of this.

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:40.030
Conversely, the average Googler would have
received $571,000 worth of their stock compensation.

00:06:40.030 --> 00:06:44.121
When you annualize these numbers along with
the base salary and bonus, you’ll see that

00:06:44.121 --> 00:06:50.160
the Googler was earning an effective salary
of $611,000 while the Amazonion was only earning

00:06:50.160 --> 00:06:55.259
$274,000, or less than half of what the compensation
appeared to be.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:59.650
Again, you can’t really complain with either
of these salaries, but you can see just how

00:06:59.650 --> 00:07:01.960
misleading Amazon’s top line number was.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:06.780
Now, I should note that Amazon sometimes offers
a larger cash bonus during year 1 to make

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.590
up for some of this gap, but that only goes
so far and doesn’t apply to everyone.

00:07:10.590 --> 00:07:13.259
Hearing this, you might be saying, why not
just stick around 4 years?

00:07:13.259 --> 00:07:15.910
Then, you’ll get the full promised amount
right?

00:07:15.910 --> 00:07:21.379
Well, the reality is that this is extremely
difficult due the axe AKA PIPs.

00:07:21.379 --> 00:07:26.750
PIPs stands for performance improvement plans
and Amazon swears by these.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:31.740
Through these plans, Amazon ranks all of their
employees every year, and then they go ahead

00:07:31.740 --> 00:07:33.240
and fire the bottom 10%.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:40.080
And as you would guess, this policy disproportionately
affects new hires and it creates an extremely

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.370
toxic work culture.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:45.190
Basically, all your team members will be trying
to backstab you so that they’re not the

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:48.360
ones who are put on a performance improvement
plan.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.409
Everyone is trying to survive the 2 year cliff
and cash in on the stock compensation that’s

00:07:52.409 --> 00:07:54.910
so close yet so far away.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:08:00.770
The reality though is that the average employee
doesn’t even pass 2 years, but let’s assume

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:04.610
that you’re part of the lucky few who make
it through the full 4 years.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:09.090
Congratulations, you got a big pay day, but
now Amazon still wants you to leave.

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:13.759
If you survive the first 4 years, it’s unlikely
that they’ll put you on a performance improvement

00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:15.479
plan and force you out that way.

00:08:15.479 --> 00:08:20.820
But, what they will do is severely nerf your
pay and hope that you either leave or stay

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:23.539
and work for substantially lower pay.

00:08:23.539 --> 00:08:24.849
Either option is ok with them.

00:08:24.849 --> 00:08:26.379
How do they do this you ask?

00:08:26.379 --> 00:08:30.319
Well, remember that massive stock grant they
offered you when you joined, well, let’s

00:08:30.319 --> 00:08:32.240
just say, you ain’t getting that again.

00:08:32.240 --> 00:08:36.570
If you’re lucky you may get stock refreshers
but that’s usually nothing in comparison

00:08:36.570 --> 00:08:39.200
to your compensation in year 3 and 4.

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:43.400
If you were L7, for example, your compensation
will drop from nearly a million dollars down

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:44.930
to $300,000.

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:48.990
And you’re expected to maintain the same
level of output if you want to continue avoiding

00:08:48.990 --> 00:08:49.990
the PIPs.

00:08:49.990 --> 00:08:54.180
By this point though, most remaining Amazonions
don’t want to put up with this anymore,

00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:58.390
so if the PIPs didn’t them, the nerfed pay
usually does.

00:08:58.390 --> 00:09:03.649
A TURNING TIDE?:
If things are so unfair at Amazon though,

00:09:03.649 --> 00:09:05.070
why do people still join?

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:09.270
Well, for delivery drivers and warehouse workers,
it’s often the only job they’re able to

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.270
find.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:11.399
But, what about the tech workers.

00:09:11.399 --> 00:09:15.160
Surely, they’re smart enough to see through
these tricks and do their research right?

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:16.160
Well, they are.

00:09:16.160 --> 00:09:20.820
Most of them know all about this, but they
choose to join anyway because Amazon is oftentimes

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:23.200
the only FAANG job that they were able to
land.

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:27.520
Of course, they could go ahead and join a
respectable background company like Visa or

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:28.520
Oracle.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.710
But, that won’t make the same impact on
their career trajectory that Amazon would.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:37.870
You see, no tech worker is putting up with
all of this to work on Amazon retail.

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:42.800
All the hype for tech workers is to get into
AWS or Amazon Web Services.

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:47.360
If you’re not familiar with AWS, they’re
the gold standard when it comes to cloud computing

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:48.459
and infrastructure.

00:09:48.459 --> 00:09:54.829
AWS holds a marketshare of 41.5% in the cloud
computing market which is more than their

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:57.270
main competitors combined.

00:09:57.270 --> 00:10:02.839
Microsoft comes in at 29.4% while Google comes
in at a measly 2.6%.

00:10:02.839 --> 00:10:08.140
As such, everyone in the cloud space is looking
for ex AWS employees.

00:10:08.140 --> 00:10:13.550
In fact, many job posting will literally list
AWS experience as a minimum qualification.

00:10:13.550 --> 00:10:18.770
So, many tech workers figure that putting
up with Amazon is their best shot at potentially

00:10:18.770 --> 00:10:22.050
landing a job at a non abusive FAANG company.

00:10:22.050 --> 00:10:25.470
And more times than not, this actually does
work out.

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:29.930
Many of the people who leave Amazon after
1.84 years aren’t actually getting fired

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:30.930
through PIP.

00:10:30.930 --> 00:10:35.100
Rather, they were offered an opportunity to
join Apple or Google’s cloud departments.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:39.899
And if you want any proof regarding just how
badly these people wanna leave Amazon just

00:10:39.899 --> 00:10:43.570
look at all the money they’re leaving on
the table in year 3 and 4.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.390
Amazon doesn’t take this as an insult though.

00:10:45.390 --> 00:10:49.490
They’re happy to be a springboard for other
FAANG companies, and at the end of the day,

00:10:49.490 --> 00:10:52.820
they’re the ones who get the most labor
for the cheapest price.

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:57.510
And within the tech side of Amazon, I don’t
think this will be changing anytime soon,

00:10:57.510 --> 00:11:00.610
but it may be changing on the blue collar
side.

00:11:00.610 --> 00:11:04.649
You see, when you’re a small company and
you’re churning through a bunch of employees,

00:11:04.649 --> 00:11:06.290
it’s not really a big deal.

00:11:06.290 --> 00:11:10.650
But, when you’re at Amazon’s size with
over a million employees, you actually run

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:13.089
the risk of running out of people to churn
through.

00:11:13.089 --> 00:11:19.320
And ironically, this is the exact crisis that
Amazon has been facing since mid 2021.

00:11:19.320 --> 00:11:23.200
According to leaked internal research, Amazon
will run out warehouse workers to churn through

00:11:23.200 --> 00:11:27.130
by 2024 which at this point is only 8 months
away.

00:11:27.130 --> 00:11:32.660
A looming recession may give Amazon more time,
but sooner or later, they’ll have to confront

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.500
the consequences of churning through so many
people.

00:11:35.500 --> 00:11:40.990
THE STATE OF AMAZON:
In the end, the reason Amazon wants their

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:45.180
employees to resign simply comes down to productivity
and financials.

00:11:45.180 --> 00:11:49.250
They believe that the most productive employees
are the ones that have just joined and have

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:50.410
something to prove.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:54.709
So, they structure all of the compensation
plans and corporate performance policies around

00:11:54.709 --> 00:11:59.160
milking out these first few years of productivity
for as cheap as possible.

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:03.220
Everything from their compensation policy
and vesting schedule to performance improvement

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:08.250
plans and work culture is all designed to
get engineers to join but not stay for long.

00:12:08.250 --> 00:12:13.310
By this point, most engineers are well aware
of the harsh reality of Amazon, but Amazon

00:12:13.310 --> 00:12:17.300
is able to leverage the reputation of AWS
to continue recruiting engineers.

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:21.870
On the warehouse side of things though, they’re
up for a massive challenge not too long from

00:12:21.870 --> 00:12:22.870
now.

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:26.709
But, only time will tell how they handle this
situation and that’s why Amazon wants their

00:12:26.709 --> 00:12:28.070
employees to resign.

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:31.160
Did you know about these clever, yet disgusting
tactics?

